+
+Network Interface
+=================
+
+Instead of a TTY, DbC can present a network interface, dbcN, that carries
+raw IP packets between the target and the debug host. This needs
+USB_XHCI_DBGCAP_NET. A kernel built without USB_XHCI_DBGCAP_TTY has only
+this function, and needs no TTY layer for DbC.
+
+When both functions are built, the xhci_hcd.dbc_net module parameter
+picks one. It is read when a controller is set up, so set it before the
+controller binds, or rebind it afterwards::
+
+	root@target: echo 1 > /sys/module/xhci_hcd/parameters/dbc_net
+	root@target: echo 0000:00:14.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/xhci_hcd/unbind
+	root@target: echo 0000:00:14.0 > /sys/bus/pci/drivers/xhci_hcd/bind
+
+Enable DbC with the dbc attribute, as for the TTY. The interface has
+carrier while DbC is configured::
+
+	root@target: ip addr add 192.168.77.1/24 dev dbc0
+	root@target: ip link set dbc0 up
+
+The debug device enumerates on the debug host as idVendor=1d6b,
+idProduct=0012, so that the debug host can tell it from ttyDBC. There,
+the dbcnet driver (USB_NET_DBCNET) binds it and presents an interface of
+its own::
+
+	root@host: ip addr add 192.168.77.2/24 dev dbc0
+	root@host: ip link set dbc0 up
+	root@host: ping 192.168.77.1
+
+Each end defaults to an MTU of 1500, and can go up to 65528 bytes on the
+target. Each packet is framed with a 4-byte header: the magic 0xdb5e and
+the payload length, both big-endian. Frames form a byte stream in each
+direction; transfer boundaries carry no meaning. A debug host without
+dbcnet can implement this in userspace, for example with libusb. It must
+end each transfer whose length is a multiple of the endpoint's maximum
+packet size with a zero-length packet.
